Each render pass goes to the GPU as soon as it is issued, and per-pass timing on request.

Panfrost keeps every pass's batch pending until something forces a submit, so with PPSSPP's 24 passes a frame the
GPU idled while the CPU issued the rest of the frame. A glFlush after each render pass hands it over at once:
OutRun 2006 on the RG DS drew 27% more frames (3284 against 2575 in the same 90 s race), its lighter road at
17 ms a drawn frame. RGDS_GL_FLUSH_PASSES=0 restores upstream's behaviour.

RGDS_GL_PASS_PROFILE=N (off by default, a measurement aid): PPSSPP's own GL profiler (timer queries around every
step, kept off upstream after Android hangs) runs with three rotating query sets, and every N frames (300 when N
is not a number) prints to stderr the average time of each step keyed by its position, size and command count,
the frame's total time issuing the steps' commands, the profiler's own time in the query reads, the submission
thread's time in the GL calls, its wait for the frame slot's fence, and one frame's step list. The CPU clock
around each step (its "issue" time) is the useful number: on Panfrost's job-manager GPUs the timestamp job sits
in the vertex chain of a fresh batch, so stamp deltas come out in any order and are printed signed.
RGDS_GL_PASS_FINISH=1 drains the GPU before each end stamp; it crashed the handheld and must not be used there.

